.TH TRK 1 "2026-10-08" "trk 0.2.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
trk \- adhd compliant task tracking
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B trk
.RI [ global\-options ]
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ arguments ...]
.PP
With no command,
.B trk
prints the status view: what you are doing, and why. With a command it runs that
command. Every command and its arguments are listed under
.BR COMMANDS .
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B trk
tracks what you are doing, and why, while your work branches into side quests.
It targets rabbit holes: you start a task, discover you need another, then
another, and three hours later you cannot remember why you came here.
.B trk
keeps the chain.
.PP
Most task trackers assume you know the full list of steps up front. Real work
does not behave that way: you begin with a goal, take one step, the step
reveals a dependency, the dependency reveals a question, and the question sends
you down a hole. By the time you climb out, you have forgotten the shape of the
thing you were originally doing.
.B trk
is built for that moment. It treats work as a tree of tasks under a goal, keeps
a pointer to the one task you are actually on, and makes it trivial to record,
in the instant, the reason a new task exists.
.PP
The tool is deliberately small in surface area and large in discipline: one
binary and one plain text file. That file is human readable, hand editable, safe
to keep in git, and safe to back up with any tool you already trust. There is no
time tracking, no network access, no accounts, no telemetry, no background
daemon, no multi\-user support, and no natural\-language date parsing beyond the
small, documented grammar in
.BR "trk at" .
.PP
The most important command is the one you type most often: bare
.BR trk .
It always answers the same question, "what was I doing, and why?", and every
other command exists to keep that answer accurate.
.SH CONCEPTS
A handful of words mean exactly one thing each throughout this manual.
.TP
.B Goal
A named "big thing" you are working toward. It has a title, a status (open or
done), and its own tree of tasks. Exactly one goal is active at a time. You do
not "do" a goal directly; you finish its tasks and then close it.
.TP
.B Task
A single line of text with a state, a creation time, optional note lines,
optional stop reason, optional schedule, and an ordered list of child tasks.
Tasks nest; a child is "something I need in order to finish the parent".
.TP
.B Tree
The tasks of a goal. The roots are the top\-level tasks, in order, and every
task has an ordered list of children. Because tasks own their children the
structure is a tree and never a graph: a task has exactly one parent, or is a
root.
.TP
.B Cursor
The pointer to the current task. Each goal stores its own cursor, which is
either a task id or empty. A cursor never points at a done or dropped task.
.TP
.B "Current task"
The task the cursor points at in the active goal, that is, "what I am doing
right now". Many commands act on it by default.
.TP
.B State
One of open, done, dropped, stopped. Stopped means "left unfinished on purpose,
with a reason". Open and stopped tasks are both unfinished, which matters for
blocking and for numbering. Done and dropped tasks are finished.
.TP
.B "Open descendant"
A task below some task that is open or stopped. "Has open children" in this
manual means "has at least one open descendant", at any depth.
.TP
.B Note
Free text attached to a task, zero or more lines. The "why" captured when a task
is created is the first note line. It is one field, called
.B why
on the command line and shown as
.B why:
in views.
.TP
.B Inbox
A goal\-less list of quick captures. Items are not tasks until you take them
into a goal.
.TP
.B Store
The flat text file holding all goals, tasks, and the inbox. See
.B FILES
for its location and
.B FILE FORMAT
for its grammar.
.TP
.B Config
The small TOML file that says where the store lives and sets a few display
options. See
.BR CONFIGURATION .
.SH THE MODEL
A goal is a container and a boundary. Only one goal is active at a time, meaning
"the goal whose tasks you are looking at". Switching goals is cheap and does not
disturb the others, because every goal remembers its own cursor; when you come
back you resume exactly where you left off.
.PP
Tasks live inside exactly one goal and never move between goals. If you always
add a child when you discover a prerequisite, then at any moment the path from
the goal down to your current task is a literal list of reasons, each one a step
on the way to the goal. That path is the why chain, and it is the thing
.B trk
is really for.
.PP
Four moves cover almost everything that happens mid\-work:
.TP
.B "trk add X"
"I need X to finish this." Create a child of the current task and descend into
it. This is the single most important command for capturing a rabbit hole.
.TP
.B "trk by X"
"Also, X." Create an unrelated top\-level task and stay where you are.
.TP
.B "trk and X"
"And also, X, at this same level." Create a sibling immediately after the
target and stay.
.TP
.B "trk then X"
"Once this is done, then X." Wrap the current branch in a new parent and stay.
This is how you build a plan forward from the first step; see
.BR "trk then" .
.PP
A fifth move, finishing with
.BR "trk done" ,
walks you back up the chain and tells you where you are and why. Because a
parent cannot be completed while it still has open descendants, the tool
refuses to let you call something "done" while loose ends remain; it lists the
outstanding children and exits 3. When you truly mean to finish a parent anyway,
say so explicitly with
.BR \-\-force .
Goals are closed in the same spirit:
.B "trk goal done"
refuses while tasks remain open unless forced.
.PP
Because the cursor is per goal and never rests on a finished task,
.B trk
always has an answer to "where am I?" that is a live, unfinished task, or an
honest "nowhere, this goal is finished".
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-color auto|always|never
Force color on or off, overriding the
.B color
key in the config file. The default is
.BR auto ,
which enables color only when standard output is a terminal,
.B NO_COLOR
is unset, and
.B TERM
is not
.BR dumb .
This option wins over the config file.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print help for
.B trk
or for the subcommand that precedes it, wrapped at 80 columns.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.PP
Two conventions apply to every command:
.IP \(bu 2
Free text is collected from all remaining positional arguments and joined with
single spaces. Quotes are optional. To pass text that starts with a
.BR \- ,
put
.B \-\-
before it.
.IP \(bu 2
An unknown subcommand is a usage error and exits 2.
.B trk
never treats an unknown word as new task text.
.SH COMMAND ALIASES
Several commands have a one\-letter alias so common actions cost one keystroke:
.RS 4
.nf
d = done    u = undo    l = list    n = note
w = why     s = stop    g = goal    i = jot
.fi
.RE
.PP
So
.B "trk u"
is
.BR "trk undo" ,
and
.B "trk g new X"
is
.BR "trk goal new X" .
.SH COMMANDS
Each command is described with its behavior, its options, and examples where
they help. Examples assume the store from
.BR "A WORKED SESSION" .
The option
.B \-s
(also spelled
.BR \-p )
chooses a target task in a picker, and is called out wherever it applies.
.SS trk
With no arguments,
.B trk
prints the status view and exits 0, even when there is no goal. This is the
command you will run more than any other.
.PP
The status view shows the goal title, up to four ancestors of the current task
(outermost first, with a summary line if there are more), the current task
marked with the cursor symbol, its why lines, a stopped line if it is stopped,
and up to eight lines of open descendants with a summary if there are more.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
Ship login fix
  Document the process
    Fix login bug
      Reproduce on staging
        > Get staging creds
          why: can't log in
.fi
.RE
.PP
If the active goal has no current task, it prints the goal title and
.BR "no current task" ,
followed by a suggestion: if open tasks exist,
.BR "try: trk switch" ;
otherwise
.BR "try: trk goal done or trk by <task>" .
If no goal is active, it prints
.BR "no goal yet. try: trk goal new <title>" .
On a brand\-new store it prints a short hint instead.
.SS trk add
.nf
trk add [\-s|--switch] [\-n|--stay] [\-w \fIWHY\fR] [--no\-why] \fITEXT\fR ...
.fi
.PP
"I need this to finish what I am doing." Creates a new open task and appends it
as the last child of the target. By default the cursor moves onto the new task,
so you descend into what you just discovered.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-switch
Choose the parent task in a picker instead of using the current task. With an
explicit parent chosen this way the cursor does not move.
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-stay
Do not move the cursor onto the new task, even though the current task is the
parent. Useful when recording a prerequisite you do not intend to start yet.
.TP
.B \-w, \-\-why \fIWHY\fP
Supply the why inline and skip the interactive prompt.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-why
Skip the why prompt and store nothing.
.PP
If the active goal has no current task, the new task becomes a root and becomes
the current task, whatever the other options say.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk add Reproduce on staging \-w "need a failing case"
Ship login fix
  Fix login bug
    > Reproduce on staging
        why: need a failing case
.fi
.RE
.SS trk by
.nf
trk by [\-w \fIWHY\fR] [--no\-why] \fITEXT\fR ...
.fi
.PP
"Also, this other thing." Creates a new open task and appends it as the last
root of the active goal. The cursor does not move, unless it was empty, in which
case the new task becomes current. Prints
.B added: <text>
followed by the status view. Contrast with
.BR add ,
which buries the new task under the current one.
.TP
.B \-w, \-\-why \fIWHY\fP
Supply the why inline.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-why
Skip the why prompt.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk by Call internet company \-w "wifi flaky"
added: Call internet company
.fi
.RE
.SS trk then
.nf
trk then [\-s|--switch] [\-w \fIWHY\fR] [--no\-why] \fITEXT\fR ...
.fi
.PP
"Once I am done with this, then that." Creates a new task and uses it to wrap
the branch you are on: the new task becomes the parent of the outermost task in
the branch containing the target, so the whole branch moves underneath it. The
cursor does not move, unless it was empty. Think of it as stretching a new frame
around the work you have already described.
.PP
This is how you build a plan outward from the first step instead of backward
from a final goal. Each
.B then
adds the next thing to do as the new outer task, and because that task is a
parent, finishing the step beneath it walks you up the chain in order. Repeated
.B then
therefore produces a list that reads top to bottom while your cursor stays on
the first step.
.PP
The difference from
.B add
is direction.
.B add
records what you need
.I before
the current task (a child);
.B then
records what happens
.I after
it (a new parent).
.PP
For example, if you are on "Download the USB image" and run
.B trk then "Install the USB image to a flash drive"
then that becomes the parent and "Download the USB image" its child. Run
.B trk then "Install the image onto a system"
next, and it wraps the chain again, becoming the parent of "Install the USB
image to a flash drive". You stay on "Download the USB image" the whole time;
finishing it with
.B trk done
moves you to the next step, and so on up the plan.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-switch
Choose the target in a picker instead of using the current task.
.TP
.B \-w, \-\-why \fIWHY\fP
Supply the why inline.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-why
Skip the why prompt.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk then Document the process \-w "after the fix ships"
Ship login fix
  Document the process
    Fix login bug
      Reproduce on staging
        > Get staging creds
.fi
.RE
.SS trk and
.nf
trk and [\-s|--switch] [\-w \fIWHY\fR] [--no\-why] \fITEXT\fR ...
.fi
.PP
Creates a new task as a sibling of the target, placed immediately after it. The
cursor does not move. This is the "one more thing at the same level" command:
rather than start a new top\-level branch with
.BR by ,
it keeps related tasks together. Without
.B \-s
it acts on the current task, so there must be one.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-switch
Choose the target in a picker.
.TP
.B \-w, \-\-why \fIWHY\fP
Supply the why inline.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-why
Skip the why prompt.
.SS trk done
.nf
trk done [\-f|--force]
.fi
.PP
Finishes the current task and walks back up the chain. It marks the task done
with a completion timestamp, clears any stop reason, and moves the cursor to the
next sensible unfinished task:
.IP \(bu 2
Let P be the nearest ancestor that is still unfinished. If P has other open
descendants, the cursor moves to the first open leaf inside P and prints a
.B next:
message naming the leaf and the task it is still needed for.
.IP \(bu 2
If P has no other open descendants, the cursor moves to P and prints a
.B back to:
message.
.IP \(bu 2
If there is no such ancestor, the cursor moves to the next unfinished top\-level
task (wrapping around) with a
.B "next top\-level task:"
message, or becomes empty if none remain.
.IP \(bu 2
If the cursor ends empty and the goal has no open tasks,
.B trk
prints a hint to close the goal.
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-force
Finish even if the task has open descendants. With
.BR \-\-force ,
the cursor jumps to the first open leaf under the finished task.
.PP
Without
.BR \-\-force ,
a task with open descendants is blocked: nothing changes, an orange warning
lists the outstanding sub\-tasks, and the exit code is 3.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk done
back to: Reproduce on staging
  why: need a failing case
.fi
.RE
.SS trk drop
.nf
trk drop [\-f|--force] [\-w \fIWHY\fR]
.fi
.PP
Like
.BR done ,
but marks the task dropped rather than done, and follows the same cursor rules.
Dropping means "I am consciously not doing this" and is reversible with
.BR "trk undo" .
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-force
Drop even if the task has open descendants. With
.BR \-\-force ,
the entire subtree is dropped, and the cursor moves using the ancestor rules
(there is no "first open leaf under the dropped task" case).
.TP
.B \-w, \-\-why \fIWHY\fP
Append the text to the dropped task's note as
.BR "dropped: <text>" .
.SS trk stop
.nf
trk stop [\-s|--switch] \fIREASON\fR ...
.fi
.PP
Leaves the target task unfinished, with a reason: the difference between
"unfinished and forgotten" and "unfinished on purpose". Sets the state to
stopped and does not move the cursor. If no reason is given and a terminal is
available,
.B trk
asks
.BR "why stopping? " .
A stopped task counts as unfinished everywhere; it still blocks its parent and
still appears in lists and pickers. Its reason shows in the status view as
.B "⏸ stopped: <reason>"
until the cursor lands on the task again, or
.B trk go
is run, or the task is done or dropped.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-switch
Choose the task in a picker.
.SS trk go
.nf
trk go
.fi
.PP
"I am back." If the current task is stopped, print
.BR "stopped earlier: <reason>" ,
set it open, and clear the reason. Always print the why chain. It is read\-only
when the task was not stopped, and exits 4 if there is no current task.
.SS trk why
.nf
trk why [\-s|--switch]
.fi
.PP
Print the why chain: the path from the goal root to the target, each node
followed by its notes. Nodes without a note show
.B "why: (none)"
in dim text. The target is marked with the cursor symbol. Read\-only.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-switch
Show the chain for a chosen task instead of the current task.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk why
Ship login fix
  Document the process
    why: after the fix ships
    Fix login bug
      why: customers locked out
      Reproduce on staging
        why: need a failing case
        > Get staging creds
          why: can't log in
.fi
.RE
.SS trk note
.nf
trk note [\-s|--switch] [\-r \fITEXT\fR] [\-c|--clear] \fITEXT\fR ...
.fi
.PP
Read or add note lines on a task. With no text, print the target's note lines.
With text, append a new note line. Notes are the same field as the why and are
shown dim in views. After a mutation,
.B trk
prints
.B noted.
and the status view.
.TP
.B \-r, \-\-replace \fITEXT\fP
Replace all note lines with the given text.
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-clear
Remove all note lines.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-switch
Act on a chosen task.
.SS trk rename
.nf
trk rename [\-s|--switch] \fITEXT\fR ...
.fi
.PP
Replace the target's text. Output is the status view.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-switch
Choose the task in a picker.
.SS trk switch
.nf
trk switch [\fIN\fR]
.fi
.PP
Choose the current task. With
.BR N ,
select row N of the numbering shown by
.BR "trk list"
(open and stopped tasks only, depth first). Without
.BR N ,
open the picker on the active goal.
.PP
After switching, the landing rule applies: if the chosen task was stopped, it is
reopened and
.B trk
prints
.BR "stopped earlier: <reason>" .
If the chosen task has open descendants,
.B trk
prints an orange warning that it cannot be completed until they are done, but
the cursor still moves. Pressing Esc in the picker exits 5 and changes nothing.
Switching to the current task is a no\-op that still prints status.
.SS trk list
.nf
trk list [\-a|--all] [--all\-goals]
.fi
.PP
Print the whole task tree of the active goal. The first line is the goal title.
Open and stopped tasks are numbered depth first from 1, and those numbers are
what
.B "trk switch N"
accepts. Indentation is two spaces per level. Read\-only.
.TP
.B \-a, \-\-all
Also show done and dropped tasks. They display
.B x
or
.B \-
instead of a number, are dim, and never count in the numbering. A done task
that still has open descendants is always shown, dim, as context, even without
.BR \-a .
.TP
.B \-\-all\-goals
Print every goal, with its title as a heading and a blank line between goals.
Numbering restarts per goal. Numbering for
.B "switch N"
applies only to the active goal.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk list
Ship login fix
 1  Document the process
 2    Fix login bug
 3      Reproduce on staging
 4        > Get staging creds
 5      Check auth logs
 6  Call internet company
.fi
.RE
.SS trk undo
.nf
trk undo [\fIN\fR]
.fi
.PP
Restore the Nth newest snapshot from the undo ring (default 1), discarding the
N\-1 newer ones and the current state. Prints
.B "undid: <label>"
followed by the status view. If the ring is empty it prints
.B "nothing to undo"
and exits 0. If N is larger than the ring, or is 0, it exits 1.
.PP
Undo restores a snapshot literally; it does not apply the landing rule. Undo is
not itself undoable, so there is no redo. Snapshots are whole\-file copies taken
before every successful mutation and kept up to
.B undo_depth
files deep.
.SS trk goal
.nf
trk goal [show|list|new|switch|done|rename|reopen]
.fi
.PP
Manage goals. With no subcommand, behaves like
.BR "goal show" .
Every subcommand except
.B show
and
.B list
mutates the store.
.SS "trk goal show"
Print the active goal's title, id, state, creation time, and counts of open and
done tasks. Read\-only.
.SS "trk goal list"
Print a numbered list of every goal, the active one marked with the cursor
symbol, each with its open task count and
.B (done)
for completed goals. These numbers are what
.B "goal switch N"
accepts. Read\-only.
.SS "trk goal new"
.nf
trk goal new [\-n|--stay] \fITITLE\fR ...
.fi
.PP
Create an open goal with an empty tree and make it active, unless
.B \-n/\-\-stay
is given. This is how a session usually begins. Name the outcome, not the first
step.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk goal new Ship login fix
goal: Ship login fix
Ship login fix
no current task
try: trk goal done or trk by <task>
.fi
.RE
.SS "trk goal switch"
.nf
trk goal switch [\fIN\fR]
.fi
.PP
Make another open goal active. With no argument, open a picker of open goals.
.B N
is the number from
.BR "goal list" .
Output is the status view for the new active goal. Each goal keeps its own
cursor.
.SS "trk goal done"
.nf
trk goal done [\-f|--force]
.fi
.PP
Mark the active goal done. If it has open tasks and
.B \-\-force
is not given, exit 3 and list up to five of them. With
.BR \-\-force ,
the tasks stay as they are. Afterwards the next open goal in file order
(wrapping) becomes active, or none does. Prints
.B "goal done: <title>"
and the new status view.
.SS "trk goal rename"
.nf
trk goal rename \fITITLE\fR ...
.fi
.PP
Rename the active goal.
.SS "trk goal reopen"
.nf
trk goal reopen [\fIN\fR]
.fi
.PP
Reopen a done goal and make it active. With no argument, open a picker.
.B N
is the number from
.BR "goal list" .
.SS trk jot
.nf
trk jot \fITEXT\fR ...
.fi
.PP
Append an item to the inbox. There is no why prompt. It never touches any goal
or cursor, and it works with no goals at all. Prints
.BR "jotted: <text>  (inbox: N)" .
.SS trk inbox
.nf
trk inbox
.fi
.PP
List the inbox items, numbered oldest first, with their creation date.
Read\-only. When empty it prints
.BR "inbox is empty" .
.SS "trk inbox take"
.nf
trk inbox take [\fIN\fR] [--goal \fINAME\fR | --new\-goal \fITITLE\fR] [\-s|--switch] [\-a|--activate]
.fi
.PP
Take an item out of the inbox and turn it into a task.
.IP "1." 3
Choose the item:
.BR N ,
or a picker if omitted. If the inbox has exactly one item, it is used
automatically.
.IP "2." 3
Choose the goal:
.B \-\-goal
(case\-insensitive substring; exactly one open goal must match),
.B \-\-new\-goal
(create it), or interactively a picker of open goals.
.IP "3." 3
Create a task from the item text in that goal as a root, appended last.
.IP "4." 3
Ask for a why, then remove the item from the inbox.
.IP "5." 3
The active goal and cursors are unchanged unless
.B \-a/\-\-activate
is given, which makes the goal active. The empty\-cursor rule applies inside
that goal.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-switch
After the goal is chosen, pick a parent task instead of making a root.
.TP
.B \-a, \-\-activate
Make the chosen goal active.
.PP
Non\-interactive use requires
.B N
and one of
.B \-\-goal
or
.BR \-\-new\-goal .
.SS "trk inbox drop"
.nf
trk inbox drop [\fIN\fR]
.fi
.PP
Remove an item from the inbox (picker if no
.BR N ).
Prints
.BR "dropped: <text>" .
.SS trk log
.nf
trk log [\fIWHEN\fR]
.fi
.PP
Print what got finished, by day, newest first. Each day's header shows the
weekday, date, and counts of tasks done and added. Done tasks are sorted by
completion time, with the goal name in brackets right\-aligned when it fits and
on its own indented line when it does not. Notes are shown dim, first line only.
Days with nothing done print
.BR "nothing finished" .
.PP
.B when
is
.B today
(the default),
.BR yesterday ,
an integer
.B N
(the last N days including today), or
.BR YYYY\-MM\-DD .
Read\-only.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk log
Wed 2026\-10\-07   4 done, 6 added
  09:40  Write repro steps                  [Ship login fix]
         why: need a failing case
  10:15  Get staging creds                  [Ship login fix]
.fi
.RE
.SS trk at
.nf
trk at [\-s|--switch] [--clear] \fIWHEN\fR ...
.fi
.PP
Set the target's schedule. The schedule is stored as an
.B @ at=
attribute and surfaces in
.BR "trk agenda" .
.TP
.B \-\-clear
Remove the schedule.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-switch
Choose the task in a picker.
.PP
With no
.B when
and a terminal,
.B trk
asks two questions:
.BR "date (YYYY\-MM\-DD, today, tomorrow, mon..sun, +Nd): "
then
.BR "time (HH:MM, Enter for none): " .
.PP
The grammar for
.B when
is a date token followed by an optional time token:
.IP \(bu 2
Date:
.BR today ,
.BR tomorrow ,
.B mon
through
.B sun
(the next such day, not today),
.BR YYYY\-MM\-DD ,
or
.B +Nd
(N days from now).
.IP \(bu 2
Time:
.B HH:MM
(24 hour) or
.B H
/
.B HH
followed by
.B am
or
.BR pm .
.IP \(bu 2
A time alone (
.BR 15:00 ,
.BR 3pm )
means today if still in the future, otherwise tomorrow.
.PP
Anything else exits 2 with the grammar in the message.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk at tomorrow 3pm
.fi
.RE
.SS trk agenda
.nf
trk agenda
.fi
.PP
Print scheduled tasks across all goals. Sections are Overdue, then each
upcoming date in ascending order; today's still\-upcoming tasks appear under
Today. Within a date, timed tasks come first by time, then untimed tasks. Only
open and stopped tasks with an
.B at
attribute appear. Read\-only.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
Overdue
  Mon 2026\-10\-05        Renew domain                   [Sticker shop]
Today  Wed 2026\-10\-07
  15:00                 Call internet company          [Ship login fix]
Fri 2026\-10\-09
  (no time)             Fix dns record                 [Ship login fix]
.fi
.RE
.SS trk start
.nf
trk start [--focus]
.fi
.PP
Open the live, full\-screen interface meant to sit in a small tmux or wezterm
pane beside your work. It works in any terminal that supports ANSI escapes and
does not depend on a particular shell or OS.
.TP
.B \-\-focus
Start in focus view: the goal, up to four ancestors of the highlighted task,
the task, and its open descendants.
.PP
The layout is a title bar with the active goal and open count, a task pane, a
why pane for the highlighted task, a transient message line, and a footer of key
hints. The keys are:
.IP \(bu 2
.B j
/
.B Down
and
.B k
/
.B Up
move the highlight (which does not change the current task).
.IP \(bu 2
.B PgUp
/
.B PgDn
page;
.B c
jumps the highlight back to the current task.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Enter
picks the highlighted task, exactly as
.BR "trk switch" .
.IP \(bu 2
.B d
completes it as
.BR "trk done" ;
.B s
stops it and asks for a reason;
.B g
runs
.BR "trk go" ;
.B n
adds a note.
.IP \(bu 2
.B u
undoes;
.B :
opens a command line that accepts a subset of the CLI commands (the rest
report "that command is not available in the TUI");
.B Tab
toggles full and focus views;
.B w
toggles a full\-height why view;
.B r
force\-reloads;
.B ?
shows help; and
.B q
or Ctrl\-C quits.
.PP
The view polls the store and reloads automatically when another terminal
changes it, so running
.B trk add
in your main pane is reflected in the pane running
.B trk start
within about half a second (the interval is the
.B poll_ms
config key). The terminal is restored on every exit path, including after a
panic.
.SS trk config
.nf
trk config [show|path]
.fi
.PP
With no subcommand, run the interactive configuration flow (see
.BR "FIRST RUN" ).
.B "config show"
prints the effective values after environment overrides, the config file path,
the store path, and the backups folder.
.B "config path"
prints only the config file path.
.PP
With non\-interactive standard input,
.B trk config
exits 1 with a message telling you to create the file by hand or set
.BR TRK_STORE .
.SS trk prompt
.nf
trk prompt [--max \fIN\fR]
.fi
.PP
For embedding in a shell prompt. Prints the current task text, truncated to
.B \-\-max
columns (default 30) with an ellipsis, and no trailing newline. Prints nothing
if there is no current task.
.B trk prompt
never prompts, never takes the lock, never prints errors, and always exits 0,
even on failure; its target is under ten milliseconds.
.TP
.B \-\-max \fIN\fP
Maximum width in columns before truncation.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
# bash
PS1='$(trk prompt) '"$PS1"
.fi
.RE
.SS trk completions
.nf
trk completions \fISHELL\fR
.fi
.PP
Print a completion script for
.BR bash ,
.BR zsh ,
.BR fish ,
.B powershell ,
or
.BR elvish .
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk completions bash  > ~/.local/share/bash\-completion/completions/trk
$ trk completions zsh   > "${fpath[1]}/_trk"
$ trk completions fish  > ~/.config/fish/completions/trk.fish
.fi
.RE
.SH FIRST RUN
The first time you run a command that needs the store,
.B trk
checks for a config file. If there is none and
.B TRK_STORE
is unset, it runs the configuration flow before continuing. The exceptions are
.BR help ,
.BR \-h ,
.BR \-V ,
.B completions
and
.BR config .
If there is no terminal, it exits 1 and tells you how to proceed by hand.
.PP
The configuration flow asks two questions:
.RS 4
.nf
Where should trk keep your task list?
(a plain text file; you can back it up or put it in git)
path [~/.local/share/trk/tasks.trk]:

Where should trk keep its backups of the task list?
(undo snapshots; a folder, ideally somewhere you also back up)
folder [~/.local/share/trk/backups]:
.fi
.RE
.PP
Press Enter to accept each default. The store may start with
.B ~
or
.BR $HOME ,
and is expanded on load. If the store already exists and parses, the flow says
how many goals it found and uses it. If it does not exist, the parent
directories are created and an empty, valid store is written. The backups
folder must be a directory; it is created if missing and may live on another
disk or inside a synced folder. If a previous config pointed elsewhere and those
files exist, you are offered the chance to move them.
.PP
The very first time you create a task in an empty store, a short walkthrough
runs: it prints a few lines of introduction, asks
.BR "What *are* you working on?" ,
creates a goal from your answer, and then runs the original command against it.
The walkthrough runs once, ever; it only triggers when there are no goals. A
bare
.B trk
on an empty store does not run it; it prints a hint instead.
.SH A WORKED SESSION
This is a complete, realistic session from an empty store to a finished goal,
narrated at each step. It matches the acceptance scenario used to test the
program, so you can reproduce it exactly.
.PP
.I "Step 0: starting a goal."
I am about to fix a login problem. Before I touch the code I name the outcome.
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk goal new Ship login fix
goal: Ship login fix
Ship login fix
no current task
try: trk goal done or trk by <task>
.fi
.RE
I have an empty goal and no current task; the tool is telling me the next move
is to add a task.
.PP
.I "Step 1: the first task."
The first concrete thing is the bug itself. It is top level, so I use
.BR by ,
not
.BR add .
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk by Fix login bug \-w "customers locked out"
added: Fix login bug
Ship login fix
  > Fix login bug
      why: customers locked out
.fi
.RE
The empty\-cursor rule made this task current even though
.B by
normally stays put.
.PP
.I "Step 2: a prerequisite."
I cannot fix what I cannot see, so I descend.
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk add Reproduce on staging \-w "need a failing case"
Ship login fix
  Fix login bug
    > Reproduce on staging
        why: need a failing case
.fi
.RE
I am now two levels deep and the chain reads like a sentence: I am reproducing
on staging because I need a failing case, to fix the login bug, because
customers are locked out.
.PP
.I "Step 3: another prerequisite."
Reproducing needs access.
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk add Get staging creds \-w "can't log in"
Ship login fix
  Fix login bug
    Reproduce on staging
      > Get staging creds
          why: can't log in
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Step 4: an unrelated interruption."
The wifi drops. This is not part of the login fix; it is a sibling at the top.
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk by Call internet company \-w "wifi flaky"
added: Call internet company
.fi
.RE
Notice my cursor did not move. The interruption was captured without derailing
me.
.PP
.I "Step 5: a task that belongs to an earlier one."
I realize I should also check the auth logs, and that belongs to "Fix login
bug", not to my current task. I switch to the target.
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk switch 1
Ship login fix
  > Fix login bug
      why: customers locked out
$ trk add \-n Check auth logs \-w "might explain the 401s"
$ trk switch 3
.fi
.RE
I used
.B \-n
so adding the child did not drag me down into it, then moved back to where I
was.
.PP
.I "Step 6: reframing the work."
I realize the credential step is contained by a documentation step that happens
after shipping.
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk then Document the process \-w "after the fix ships"
Ship login fix
  Document the process
    Fix login bug
      Reproduce on staging
        > Get staging creds
          why: can't log in
.fi
.RE
.B then
wrapped my whole chain in the new task. My place is unchanged, but it now sits
inside a larger, correct frame.
.PP
.I "Step 7: finishing the leaf."
I have the credentials.
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk done
back to: Reproduce on staging
  why: need a failing case
.fi
.RE
.B trk
walked me up exactly one level, to the nearest unfinished ancestor, and reminded
me why it exists. If instead a sibling had still needed me, it would have said
.B next:
and named it.
.PP
.I "Step 8: climbing the chain."
I continue finishing, one sensible step at a time:
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk done    # Reproduce on staging is done; next: Check auth logs
$ trk done    # Check auth logs is done; back to Fix login bug
$ trk done    # Fix login bug is done; back to Document the process
$ trk done    # Document the process is done; next top\-level task: Call internet company
$ trk done    # Call internet company is done; goal has no open tasks
.fi
.RE
At each step the message told me exactly where I landed and why. When the goal
ran out of work,
.B trk
printed
.BR "goal has no open tasks. when you are done: trk goal done" .
.PP
.I "Step 9: undoing a mistake."
I finished the last task by accident and want it back.
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk undo
undid: done
.fi
.RE
The task is open again and current. There is no redo; if I had wanted to step
back further I would have run
.BR "trk undo 2" .
.PP
.I "Step 10: closing the goal."
When I mean it, I close the goal.
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk goal done
goal done: Ship login fix
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Step 11: the stray thought."
At some point during all this, a thought arrived that did not belong anywhere
yet.
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk jot look into sqlite wal mode
jotted: look into sqlite wal mode  (inbox: 1)
.fi
.RE
Later, when I had a spare minute, I filed it into a goal:
.RS 4
.nf
$ trk inbox take 1 \-\-goal "Ship login fix"
.fi
.RE
.SH FILE FORMAT
The store is one flat UTF\-8 text file, line oriented, written with LF and read
with CRLF tolerated. It is rewritten whole and atomically on every change; it is
not append\-only. It is never wrapped; wrapping applies only to terminal output.
.PP
A store has a header, an optional inbox, and zero or more goal blocks. Here is a
complete example:
.RS 4
.nf
trk 1
next g=2 t=7 i=1
active g1

inbox
  i3 2026\-10\-07T09:30:00\-05:00 look into sqlite wal mode

goal g1 open 2026\-10\-07T09:00:00\-05:00 Ship login fix
  current t3
  [ ] t6 2026\-10\-07T09:06:00\-05:00 Document the process
    | after the fix ships
    [ ] t1 2026\-10\-07T09:01:00\-05:00 Fix login bug
      | customers locked out
      [ ] t2 2026\-10\-07T09:02:00\-05:00 Reproduce on staging
        | need a failing case
        [ ] t3 2026\-10\-07T09:03:00\-05:00 Get staging creds
          | can't log in
      [ ] t5 2026\-10\-07T09:05:00\-05:00 Check auth logs
        | might explain the 401s
  [ ] t4 2026\-10\-07T09:04:00\-05:00 Call internet company
    | wifi flaky
.fi
.RE
.PP
The header is
.B trk
followed by the format version, then a
.B next
line with the next unused counter per kind, then an optional
.B active
line naming the active goal. Comments begin with
.B #
at column zero and are ignored and dropped on the next write.
.PP
Inside a goal, the
.B current
line names the cursor. A task line is indented two spaces per level and begins
with a bracketed state:
.B [ ]
open,
.B [x]
done,
.B [-]
dropped,
.B [!]
stopped. A done or dropped task must carry
.B @ done=
or
.B @ dropped=
with an RFC 3339 timestamp. A scheduled task carries
.B @ at=
with either a timestamp or a date. A stopped task must carry a
.B !
reason line. Notes are
.B |
lines. A goal is
.B goal g\fIN\fR open|done \fItimestamp\fR \fItitle\fR
and a completed goal adds
.B @ done=
on the next indented line.
.PP
Indentation is exactly two spaces per level; tabs are a parse error. Unknown
line types or bad structure produce a parse error with a line number;
.B trk
never guesses and never partially loads. Parsing then serializing a valid file
produces canonical text, so hand edits are allowed and normalized on the next
write.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The config file is TOML. Its location, in order, is
.BR $TRK_CONFIG ,
then
.BR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/trk/config.toml ,
then
.BR $HOME/.config/trk/config.toml .
On Windows
.B $HOME
means the user profile directory; the path is otherwise the same everywhere.
.PP
The keys are:
.TP
.B store
Required. Path to the store. May start with
.B ~
or
.BR $HOME .
.TP
.B backups
Directory for undo snapshots. Optional; defaults to a
.B backups
directory next to the store.
.TP
.B color
.BR auto | always | never .
Default
.BR auto .
.TP
.B wrap
Maximum output width in columns, clamped to the range 40 to 200. Default 80.
.TP
.B undo_depth
Number of undo snapshots kept. Default 50.
.TP
.B prompt_why
Ask
.B why?
when creating a task. Default true.
.TP
.B ascii
.BR auto | always | never .
Default
.BR auto .
.TP
.B current_color
The color of the current task: one of
.BR black ,
.BR red ,
.BR green ,
.BR yellow ,
.BR blue ,
.BR magenta ,
.BR cyan ,
.BR white ,
or a
.B bright\-
prefixed variant. Default
.BR green .
.TP
.B poll_ms
Change\-detection interval for
.BR "trk start" ,
in milliseconds. Default 500.
.PP
Unknown keys produce a warning, not an error. Invalid values exit 1 naming the
key and the file.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TRK_CONFIG
Path to the config file. Overrides the default location.
.TP
.B TRK_STORE
Path to the store. Overrides the
.B store
key in the config. If set and no config exists, the first\-run flow is skipped.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disables color.
.TP
.B TRK_ASCII
Set to
.B 1
to force ASCII symbols.
.TP
.B TRK_NOW
Undocumented; for tests only. An RFC 3339 time used as "now".
.SH OUTPUT AND SYMBOLS
Normal output goes to standard output; warnings and errors go to standard
error. Wrapping width is the smaller of
.B wrap
and the terminal width, and wrapping is display\-width aware, preserving hanging
indents.
.PP
Errors look like
.BR "trk: <lowercase message>" .
Warnings look like
.B "warning: <message>"
and are printed in orange. Orange is ANSI 256\-color 208; terminals without 256
colors get yellow. Color is on only when standard output is a terminal,
.B NO_COLOR
is unset, and
.B TERM
is not
.BR dumb ;
.B \-\-color
and
.B config.color
can force it.
.PP
Symbols have an ASCII fallback, used when the locale is not UTF\-8, or when
.B TRK_ASCII=1
or
.B ascii = "always"
is set:
.RS 4
.nf
Meaning          Unicode   ASCII
cursor             ►        >
stopped marker     ⏸        !
up/back arrow      ↑        ^
ellipsis           …     ...
.fi
.RE
.PP
The goal title is bold white. The current task is bold in
.B current_color
(default green). Every other task is faded green, so the current task stands
out; done and dropped rows are dim; notes are dim; warnings are orange; errors
are red.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
General error: I/O, parse failure, lock timeout, or an invalid value.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, including unknown subcommands and bad
.B at
grammar.
.TP
.B 3
Blocked: a task has open children and
.B \-\-force
was not given, or a goal has open tasks and
.B \-\-force
was not given.
.TP
.B 4
Nothing to act on: no active goal, or no current task.
.TP
.B 5
Cancelled by the user in a picker or prompt.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/trk/config.toml
Default config file (or under
.BR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME ).
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/trk/tasks.trk
Default store (or under
.BR $XDG_DATA_HOME ).
.TP
.I <store>.lock
Advisory lock held for the duration of a mutation; created on demand.
.TP
.I <backups>/NNNNNN.trk
Undo snapshots, each prefixed with a
.B "# undo\-label:"
comment recording the command that caused it.
.SH NOTES
.B trk
has no time tracking, no network access, sync, accounts, or telemetry, no
background daemon, no multi\-user features, no redo, and no natural\-language
date parsing beyond the grammar in
.BR "trk at" .
Future ideas, deliberately not in this version, include reordering and
reparenting, search, export, tags, repeating tasks, a calendar picker, theming,
and an edit command that opens
.BR $EDITOR .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux (1),
.BR git (1)
.PP
Run
.B trk \-\-help
or
.B trk command \-\-help
for a quick reminder of any command and its options.
